# MedSimplify
 
A Streamlit app that looks up a medication, checks for FDA recalls, and uses
the Gemini API to rewrite the official drug label in plain language.
 
## Setup
 
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
export GEMINI_API_KEY="your-key-here"   # or paste it into the sidebar at runtime
streamlit run app.py
```
 
No key for openFDA is required (it's a public API, though you can add one
for higher rate limits later if you want).
 
## How it meets the assignment requirements
 
| Requirement | Where it lives |
|---|---|
| Fetch drug info (usage, warnings, side effects, dosage) | `FDAClient.fetch_label()` → openFDA Drug Labeling API |
| Rewrite in plain language | `AITranslator.simplify()` → Gemini API |
| Check recent recalls, warn the user | `FDAClient.fetch_recalls()` + `render_recall_banner()` → openFDA Enforcement API |
| Save search history to a local file | `SearchHistory` → reads/writes `search_history.json` |
| File handling | `SearchHistory._read()` / `_write()` |
| Exception handling | `InvalidMedicationNameError`, `MedicationNotFoundError`, `FDANetworkError`, `AITranslationError` — each caught and shown to the user instead of crashing the app |
| Regular expressions | `Medication.clean_text()` strips citation markers/bullets/whitespace; `Medication.validate_name()` validates input; `Medication.extract_warning_keywords()` pulls warning phrases out of raw text |
| OOP | `Medication`, `FDAClient`, `AITranslator`, `SearchHistory` classes |
 
## Notes / things you could extend
 
- `FDAClient.fetch_label()` tries generic name, then brand name, then a
  free-text fallback — you could add fuzzy matching if a query returns nothing.
- History is capped at the 100 most recent searches in `search_history.json`.
- The Gemini model is set to `gemini-2.0-flash` in `AITranslator.__init__`;
  change the `model` argument if your assignment specifies a different one.
- No API keys are hardcoded — the Gemini key is read from an environment
